---
import SiteShell from "../../app/providers/site-shell.astro";
import Callout from "../../shared/ui/callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../shared/ui/code-block.astro";
import Heading from "../../shared/ui/heading.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../shared/ui/page-header.astro";
import TextLink from "../../shared/ui/text-link.astro";
---

<SiteShell
  title="Workflow"
  description="Follow the reviewable type-tune scan, plan, dry-run, and apply workflow."
  withSidebar
>
  <article class="prose">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Usage · Six steps"
      title="The workflow"
      lead="Scan first, review a stable plan, preview the rewrite, and only then keep file changes."
    />

    <p>
      Examples use <code>pnpm exec type-tune</code> after <code>pnpm add -D type-tune</code>. After
      <code>npm install -D type-tune</code>, use <code>npx type-tune</code> with the same arguments.
      Installation and the first scan are covered in
      <TextLink href="/usage/getting-started">Getting started</TextLink>.
    </p>

    <ol class="workflow-list content-section">
      <li>
        <strong>Scan candidates.</strong>
        <p>Inspect shared declarations, referrer counts, destinations, and omission reasons.</p>
        <CodeBlock code="pnpm exec type-tune scan --project tsconfig.json --verbose --explain" />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Create a plan.</strong>
        <p>
          These output paths are the defaults. Passing them explicitly makes the later apply command
          obvious.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={`pnpm exec type-tune plan \\
  --project tsconfig.json \\
  --out .type-tune/plan.json \\
  --markdown .type-tune/plan.md`} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Review <code>plan.md</code>.</strong>
        <p>
          Candidates start as <code>ignore</code>. <code>review</code> needs an interactive terminal
          and toggles each candidate between <code>apply</code> and <code>ignore</code>. You can
          edit the Markdown directly instead. Keep every candidate ID from the generated file.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code="pnpm exec type-tune review --markdown .type-tune/plan.md" />
        <CodeBlock language="markdown" label="plan.md" code={'- [apply] abc123def456 `OrderSummary`\n  - kind: `typeAlias`\n  - from: `src/features/order/schema.ts`\n  - to: `src/domains/orders/types/order-summary.ts`'} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Preview the real rewrite.</strong>
        <CodeBlock code={`pnpm exec type-tune apply \\
  --plan .type-tune/plan.json \\
  --markdown .type-tune/plan.md \\
  --dry-run`} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apply.</strong>
        <CodeBlock code={`pnpm exec type-tune apply \\
  --plan .type-tune/plan.json \\
  --markdown .type-tune/plan.md`} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the result.</strong>
        <p>
          Read the Git diff, run your formatter, and run the tests or framework build that
          <code>tsc --noEmit</code> does not cover.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading><code>plan.json</code> and <code>plan.md</code></Heading>
      <p>
        <code>plan.json</code> is the structured record: candidate identity, source and proposed
        destination, referrers, local type dependencies, import strategy, a source fingerprint, and
        the options that produced the plan. Treat it as input for apply. Do not edit its candidate
        metadata.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>plan.md</code> is the review surface. Apply reads two edits from it: the
        <code>[apply]</code> or <code>[ignore]</code> marker, and the <code>to:</code> path. Every
        candidate ID in <code>plan.json</code> must still appear exactly once, and each entry needs
        a <code>to:</code> line. Applying without Markdown requires
        <code>--accept-unreviewed</code>, which uses the statuses stored in <code>plan.json</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before writing files, apply scans again and rejects a stale fingerprint, a missing
        declaration, a duplicate candidate, or a path outside the project.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Destination rules</Heading>
      <p>
        The default <code>--placement-style</code> is <code>collocation</code>, which proposes a
        <code>types/</code> file beside the source. <code>repo-types</code> proposes the equivalent
        path under the repository <code>types/</code> directory. Either proposal can be replaced by
        editing <code>to:</code> in <code>plan.md</code>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The path is project-relative, uses forward slashes, and has no <code>.</code> or <code>..</code> segments.</li>
        <li>It ends in <code>.ts</code>, <code>.tsx</code>, <code>.mts</code>, or <code>.cts</code>, and it is not a declaration file.</li>
        <li>It differs from the source path, and the destination file does not already exist.</li>
        <li>Approved candidates from the same source file share one destination.</li>
        <li>Two different declarations with the same symbol name are not written to the same destination.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>What the rewrite guarantees</Heading>
      <p>
        For approved entries, type-tune moves the declaration and its safe same-file type
        dependencies, updates supported type imports, and validates with
        <code>tsc --noEmit --project</code>. Relative imports, <code>baseUrl</code> and
        <code>paths</code> aliases, and simple barrel re-exports are updated when they can be
        resolved. The original module keeps a type-only compatibility re-export when that is needed
        to preserve an unresolved star barrel, an external package import, or a consumer the scan
        does not rewrite.
      </p>
      <p>
        A move that would update a Vue script referrer, or that encounters a Vue file type-tune
        could not analyze, is rejected before any file is written. <code>--dry-run</code> uses that
        same gate. A normal TypeScript failure rolls the file changes back. <code>--dry-run</code>
        performs the same write and validation, then restores the files when that process finishes
        normally.
        Killing the process, or editing the same files at the same time, is outside that guarantee.
        If rollback itself fails, the CLI reports the failure instead of claiming the tree was
        restored.
      </p>
      <Callout>
        <code>tsc</code> does not typecheck Vue, Svelte, or Astro files, and it does not see
        consumers outside this project. Run those builds and tests after a move. Formatter-clean
        output is not guaranteed.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Optional AI review</Heading>
      <p>
        AI review runs only when <code>plan --ai-review</code> is set. It can annotate a candidate
        with <code>apply</code>, <code>ignore</code>, or <code>needs-review</code>. That note does
        not change source files, and <code>apply</code> does not call a provider.
      </p>
      <p>
        The default context mode is <code>snippets</code>, which includes declaration text, local
        type dependencies, and relevant import lines. <code>summary</code> sends metadata and paths.
        <code>full</code> also sends the involved source files. Redaction covers common secret
        assignments only as a best effort.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={`pnpm exec type-tune plan \\
  --project tsconfig.json \\
  --ai-review \\
  --ai-dry-run \\
  --ai-request-out .type-tune/ai-request.json`} />
      <p>
        <code>--ai-dry-run</code> does not contact a provider. A later review with OpenAI,
        OpenAI-compatible, or Anthropic sends that context to the provider. Ollama defaults to a
        local endpoint at <code>127.0.0.1:11434</code>. The built-in <code>mock</code> provider
        reads <code>--ai-response-file</code> and does not make a network call. Provider flags are
        listed under <TextLink href="/config/ai-review-options">AI review options</TextLink>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteShell>

<style>
  .workflow-list {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    counter-reset: workflow;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .workflow-list > li {
    position: relative;
    padding: 0 0 3rem 3.5rem;
    counter-increment: workflow;
  }

  .workflow-list > li > strong {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .workflow-list > li::before {
    content: counter(workflow, decimal-leading-zero);
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.05rem;
    left: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 2rem;
    height: 2rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .workflow-list > li:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    top: 2.35rem;
    bottom: 0.45rem;
    left: 0.9rem;
    width: 1px;
    background: var(--secondary);
  }

  .workflow-list p {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .workflow-list > li {
      padding-left: 2.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
